  </info>

  <title>Quick terminal</title>

<p>The quick terminal is a <app>Pano Terminal</app> window that you show and
  hide with one key, wherever you are. It appears at the top of the screen,
  above the other windows.</p>

  <section id="shortcut">
    <title>Choose a key</title>

    <p>On GNOME 48 or newer, and on KDE Plasma, the desktop can give
    <app>Pano Terminal</app> a system-wide shortcut:</p>
    <steps>
<item><p>Press the menu button in the top-right corner of the window and select <gui style="menuitem">Preferences</gui>.</p></item>
<item><p>In the <gui>Quick Terminal</gui> group, turn on <gui>System-Wide Shortcut</gui>.</p></item>
<item><p>The desktop asks you for the key. <key>F12</key> is suggested.</p></item>
</steps>

    <p>The desktop sends the shortcut only to an application that runs, so
    <app>Pano Terminal</app> then keeps running in the background, and starts
    when you log in.</p>

    <p>On other desktops, add a custom shortcut in the keyboard settings of
    your desktop that runs this command, which shows or hides the quick
    terminal:</p>
    <code>pano-terminal --quick</code>
    <p>On GNOME, this is in <guiseq><gui>Settings</gui><gui>Keyboard</gui>
    <gui>View and Customize Shortcuts</gui><gui>Custom Shortcuts</gui></guiseq>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="settings">
    <title>Size and behavior</title>

    <p>The <gui>Quick Terminal</gui> group of the preferences also sets:</p>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Width</gui> and <gui>Height</gui></title>
        <p>The size of the quick terminal, in percent of the screen.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Hide When Another App Is Used</gui></title>
        <p>Hides the quick terminal when another application gets the
        focus.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Slide In From the Top</gui></title>
        <p>Slides the quick terminal in from the top edge of the screen.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>

  <section id="wayland">
    <title>Wayland</title>

    <p>On X11, <app>Pano Terminal</app> places the quick terminal at the top of
    the screen and keeps it above the other windows by itself. On Wayland,
    applications cannot place their windows. On GNOME, the
    <app>Pano Terminal Quick Terminal</app> extension of GNOME Shell, which
    comes with <app>Pano Terminal</app>, does that: turn on
    <gui>GNOME Shell Extension</gui> in the <gui>Quick Terminal</gui> group.
    It then also stays on all workspaces. You may have to log out and in again
    after installing <app>Pano Terminal</app> before the extension can be
    turned on.</p>
  </section>

